  planets:
  - id: 0
    displayName: Mercury
    kind: Terrestrial planet
    diameterKm: 4879
    meanDistanceMillionKm: 57.9
    orbitalPeriodDays: 88
    description: The smallest planet and the closest to the Sun. Its heavily cratered surface records a long history of impacts.
  - id: 1
    displayName: Venus
    kind: Terrestrial planet
    diameterKm: 12104
    meanDistanceMillionKm: 108.2
    orbitalPeriodDays: 224.7
    description: A thick atmosphere hides Venus's surface. Its intense greenhouse effect makes it the hottest planet.
  - id: 2
    displayName: Earth
    kind: Terrestrial planet
    diameterKm: 12756
    meanDistanceMillionKm: 149.6
    orbitalPeriodDays: 365.2
    description: Our home planet has liquid water at the surface and is the only known world with life.
  - id: 3
    displayName: Mars
    kind: Terrestrial planet
    diameterKm: 6792
    meanDistanceMillionKm: 228
    orbitalPeriodDays: 687
    description: The red planet has a thin atmosphere, polar ice, and evidence that liquid water flowed there long ago.
  - id: 4
    displayName: Jupiter
    kind: Gas giant
    diameterKm: 142984
    meanDistanceMillionKm: 778.5
    orbitalPeriodDays: 4331
    description: The largest planet is a gas giant with broad cloud bands and the long-lived Great Red Spot storm.
  - id: 5
    displayName: Saturn
    kind: Gas giant
    diameterKm: 120536
    meanDistanceMillionKm: 1432
    orbitalPeriodDays: 10747
    description: Saturn is a gas giant best known for its extensive rings, made mostly of ice and rocky debris.
  - id: 6
    displayName: Uranus
    kind: Ice giant
    diameterKm: 51118
    meanDistanceMillionKm: 2867
    orbitalPeriodDays: 30589
    description: Uranus is an ice giant with a blue-green appearance and an unusually tilted rotation axis.
  - id: 7
    displayName: Neptune
    kind: Ice giant
    diameterKm: 49528
    meanDistanceMillionKm: 4515
    orbitalPeriodDays: 59800
    description: The most distant planet is a dark blue ice giant with powerful winds and dynamic storms.
